{
  "image_name": "1fec34ab3593d751beb88cba6f3e1062.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts how different fatty acids may shape T-cell behavior in an atherosclerotic plaque, promoting either disease progression or protection. In both cases, a quiescent T cell initially relies on OXPHOS, then appears to switch metabolism during activation.\n\nThe upper half shows a pro-inflammatory pathway associated with several saturated fatty acids and some unsaturated fatty acids. These lipids are linked to differentiation toward Th1 and Th17-like cells, marked by transcription factors such as T-bet and RORγt. These cells produce inflammatory cytokines including TNF, IFNγ, IL-2, and IL-17A, which increase pro-inflammatory mediators, stimulate cell proliferation, and are shown leading to worsening atherosclerosis.\n\nThe lower half shows an anti-inflammatory pathway associated with other MUFAs and omega-3-type PUFAs. Here, T cells are directed toward Th2 and Treg phenotypes, labeled by GATA3 and FOXP3. These cells produce IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, and TGFβ, increasing anti-inflammatory mediators and proliferation in a way that is depicted as atheroprotective.\n\nOverall, the graphic emphasizes that lipid type influences T-cell metabolic programming, immune polarization, and whether the plaque environment becomes more inflammatory or more protective.",
